-- transaction itself and implements --dry-run by discarding it. An inline transaction terminator in
-- this file would silently turn a dry run into a real prod write.
--
-- DOCUMENT IDENTITY (checked on the cover, national foreword and endorsement notice before quoting)
--   BS EN ISO 14015:2022, "Environmental management - Guidelines for environmental due diligence
--     assessment". Published 31 July 2022 by BSI Standards Limited. ISBN 978 0 539 05465 1.
--     ICS 13.020.10.
--   National foreword (PDF p.2): "This British Standard is the UK implementation of EN ISO
--     14015:2022. It is identical to ISO 14015:2022. It supersedes BS EN ISO 14015:2010, which is
--     withdrawn."
--   Endorsement notice (PDF p.4): "The text of ISO 14015:2022 has been approved by CEN as
--     EN ISO 14015:2022 without any modification."
--   => The ISO edition implemented is ISO 14015:2022 (2nd edition; the 1st was ISO 14015:2001,
--     adopted in the UK as BS EN ISO 14015:2010). Prod records version "2022 (BS EN ISO
--     14015:2022; identical to ISO 14015:2022; supersedes ISO 14015:2001)" - CORRECT and MATCHING.
--   THIS IS THE PUBLISHED STANDARD - not a Draft/DIS/FDIS, not a Technical Report, not a scoping
--     report. No ballot notice, no "shall not be cited as a standard" caution, no draft-stage
--     marking anywhere in the 39-page text layer.
--   Language: English (the CEN title page also carries the French and German titles; the body is
--     English only).
--
-- HEADLINE FINDING - THE DOCUMENT CONTAINS NO "SHALL" AT ALL
--   Mechanical modality census over the extracted text layer:
--     BSI/CEN/ISO front matter (PDF pp.1-10):   shall=4   should=4    may=10  can=8
--     BODY, printed pp.1-26 (PDF pp.11-36):     shall=0   should=84   may=39  can=41  must=1
--       - Clauses 1-3 (Scope/Refs/Terms):       shall=0   should=0    may=7   can=10
--       - Clauses 4-7 (the normative body):     shall=0   should=84   may=26  can=30  must=1
--       - Annex A (informative):                shall=0   should=0    may=1   can=1
--       - Annex B (informative):                shall=0   should=0    may=5   can=0
--   The four "shall" hits are ALL national-adoption / patent-rights boilerplate in the front
--   matter ("This European Standard shall be given the status of a national standard...",
--   "conflicting national standards shall be withdrawn...", "CEN shall not be held responsible
--   for identifying any or all such patent rights", "ISO shall not be held responsible..."). None
--   of them imposes anything on an EDD assessment. The single "must" is descriptive, inside an
--   example: "time period within which costs must be met (e.g. related to likelihood of
--   enforcement activities...)" (printed p.15).
--   Shall:should ratio in the normative body = 0 : 84.
--   => NO BLOCK GATE IS DEFENSIBLE ON THIS STANDARD. Every requirement in ISO 14015:2022 is a
--      "should", a "may" or a "can". The encoding already reflects this: all 34 encoded gates are
--      severity='warn' and NOT ONE is 'block'. That is the correct outcome and this pack changes
--      nothing about it. Conversely, no future pass should promote any of these gates to block.
--      The consequence for the product is that an ISO-14015 worksheet can never hard-fail: the
--      standard is advisory by construction and the encoding is honest about that.
--
-- Grade: [VA] - the source is the rendered PDF's own text layer, which under SR-3 is ground truth.
--   Source PDF : C:\Users\Ekowai\Desktop\Ciruclar economy, sustanability and water test\
--                ISO 14015-2022-BS-EN-ISO-Environmental-Management-Guidelines.pdf
--   Text layer : pdftotext -layout -enc UTF-8, form feeds preserved, 39 pages / 111 521 bytes.
--   OCR CAUTION (honest residue, not a footnote): the extraction reported 6 undecodable
--     characters. All 6 sit on PDF p.10, inside the Figure 1 flow diagram, which the extractor
--     renders as unreadable line-art debris ("-C-- lient (5.2.2)---1rT"). NOTHING is quoted from
--     PDF p.10 and no quote in this pack contains U+FFFD - the generator asserts this per row.
--     Beyond those 6, the text layer is OCR-derived and carries visible character damage
--     elsewhere: "Annex 8" for "Annex B", "Annex e" for "Annex C", "Table 8.1" for "Table B.1",
--     "identifyelements", "virtualjremote", "interview ing", "signif icance", "v isit", "o f",
--     "a n", "(ll)" for cross-reference numbers, "ISO 140 3 1" for "ISO 14031". Every quote below
--     was chosen from a CLEAN run; where the sentence that most directly defines a field is
--     damaged, the row says so in verification_note and quotes an undamaged sentence that carries
--     the same obligation, rather than repairing the text. No quote was silently corrected.
--     NOTE ON THE SPOTCHECK: spotcheck-pack.mjs proves a quote is a byte-exact substring of THIS
--     extraction. It cannot prove the extraction is faithful to the printed page; that is why
--     damaged runs are excluded by hand rather than trusted to the checker.
--
-- PAGE CONVENTION (established by reading the running footer on every page; the BSI wrapper adds
-- front and back matter, so the offset differs between the roman and arabic sequences)
--   text page N = PDF page N (form feeds preserved).
--   Roman front matter: PDF p.4 = ii, p.5 = iii (Contents), p.6 = iv, p.7 = v (ISO Foreword),
